Both pairs are off by exactly one ULP, the smallest possible difference between two doubles.

```js
Math.abs(0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3) < Number.EPSILON;
// true

Math.abs(1234567.89 + 9876543.21 - 11111111.1) < Number.EPSILON;
// false  (the difference is 1.862645149230957e-9)
```

questions.explanation:
Both results are equally accurate: each is one step away from the expected value. A fixed threshold still passes one and fails the other, which is exactly why the technique doesn't work.

One ULP near 0.3 is 2⁻⁵⁴ ≈ 5.6 × 10⁻¹⁷, well under `Number.EPSILON`. One ULP near 1.1 × 10⁷ is 2⁻²⁹ ≈ 1.86 × 10⁻⁹, 8,388,608 times bigger than `Number.EPSILON`. The threshold stayed the same. The numbers got bigger, so the gaps between doubles got bigger too.

A tolerance has to scale with the size of the values:

```js
function closeEnough(a, b, ulps = 4) {
  const scale = Math.max(Math.abs(a), Math.abs(b));
  return Math.abs(a - b) <= ulps * Number.EPSILON * scale;
}
```

Even this needs an absolute minimum near zero, where relative error stops making sense.

For money, no tolerance is correct. If a comparison only has to be approximately right, it shouldn't be done on floats.

